- **1% to 3%** reply rate on managed cold email, with 20%+ of replies positive (Source: [Myntmore Benchmark Report](/resources/blogs/b2b-outbound-benchmark-report-2026))
- **8 touches** average number needed before a prospect engages (Source: RAIN Group)
- **49%** of B2B buyers research vendors on LinkedIn before responding (Source: LinkedIn Research)

## The definition, in one sentence

Outbound sales means initiating contact with prospective buyers who fit your ideal customer profile, rather than waiting for them to find your website, read your content, or reach out to your sales team first.

## The core channels of modern outbound

### Cold Email

Reaching prospects via verified business email. Scalable and cost-effective when run with proper technical infrastructure and targeted messaging.

[See the service](/services/cold-email)

### LinkedIn Outreach

Building relationships through personalized connection requests and direct messages on LinkedIn. Higher touch, warmer context, and effective for senior decision-makers.

[See the service](/services/linkedin-outreach)

### Account-Based Marketing (ABM)

Outbound aimed at a short list of named target companies rather than a broad list, multi-threading several stakeholders inside each account instead of messaging one contact and hoping.

[See the service](/services/account-based-marketing)

## The anatomy of an outbound system

Whatever the channel, a working outbound system moves through the same six stages:

1. ICP. Define the exact company and buyer profile worth targeting, so effort isn't spent on prospects who were never going to buy. [Learn more →](/resources/glossary/icp)
2. List building. Compile a list of real companies and contacts matching that ICP, ideally filtered further by live buying signals, not just static firmographic fit. [Learn more →](/resources/glossary/buying-signal)
3. Messaging. Write outreach that references something specific about the recipient and leads with their problem, not a pitch for your product.
4. Sequencing. Space multiple touches, often across more than one channel, since most replies come after several attempts, not the first message.
5. Sales ops. An SDR or similar role runs the outreach day to day and hands off booked meetings to whoever closes deals. [Learn more →](/resources/glossary/sdr)
6. Pipeline. Booked meetings become a tracked pipeline, moving through stages toward closed-won, which is what the whole system exists to fill. [Learn more →](/resources/glossary/sales-pipeline)

## Building it in-house vs. outsourcing it

Most companies eventually face this decision directly: hire an SDR (or a small team) and build the infrastructure internally, or run outbound through an agency that already has the domains, tooling, and playbooks in place. There's no universally right answer, it comes down to stage, volume, and how proven the motion already is inside your company. We cover the actual tradeoffs, cost, speed, and control, in [Agency vs. In-House SDR for B2B Outbound](/resources/blogs/agency-vs-in-house). If you choose to keep execution in-house, our [DIY outbound frameworks](/services/do-it-yourself) provide step-by-step guidance. Industry-specific strategies are also available for [recruitment firms](/lp/recruitment-firms) and [manufacturers and exporters](/lp/manufacturers-exporters).

## How to know if outbound is worth it for you

Outbound tends to pay off fastest for companies that already know their ICP with some precision, sell something with a clear, explainable value proposition, and need pipeline on a predictable schedule rather than whenever inbound happens to convert. It tends to underperform when the ICP is still genuinely unclear, since no amount of sequencing or channel choice fixes a targeting problem. If that's where you are, start with [defining the ICP](/resources/glossary/icp) before building any outreach on top of it.
